WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.208 --> 00:00:03.253
- Did you know that roughly
four out of 10 marriages

00:00:03.253 --> 00:00:04.838
end in divorce?

00:00:04.838 --> 00:00:05.922
- I mean, I get it.

00:00:05.922 --> 00:00:09.050
- That number has come down
somewhat since the 1980s, but.

00:00:09.050 --> 00:00:10.093
(record scratches)

00:00:10.093 --> 00:00:12.971
Wait, what do you mean you get it?

00:00:12.971 --> 00:00:15.390
- Oh, just the things
don't always work out

00:00:15.390 --> 00:00:17.058
the way you envision.

00:00:17.058 --> 00:00:18.435
- Go on.

00:00:18.435 --> 00:00:22.272
- Well, for example, I was
fairly certain when I was little

00:00:22.272 --> 00:00:24.733
that my husband would be-

00:00:24.733 --> 00:00:25.817
- Yes?

00:00:25.817 --> 00:00:28.236
- Jonathan Taylor Thomas.

00:00:28.236 --> 00:00:29.779
But I'm glad he's not.

00:00:29.779 --> 00:00:31.990
This is so much better.

00:00:31.990 --> 00:00:33.491
- For one reason or another-

00:00:33.491 --> 00:00:34.409
- I'm pretty sure.

00:00:35.410 --> 00:00:36.786
- For one reason or another,

00:00:36.786 --> 00:00:39.330
lots of marriages simply run their course,

00:00:39.330 --> 00:00:41.124
and finding yourself suddenly single

00:00:41.124 --> 00:00:42.542
is a major life change

00:00:42.542 --> 00:00:44.586
that can turn your finances upside down.

00:00:44.586 --> 00:00:46.588
- When it comes to untangling your money

00:00:46.588 --> 00:00:49.507
and starting over, what
happens to your debt?

00:00:49.507 --> 00:00:51.134
How are your assets divided?

00:00:51.134 --> 00:00:52.886
How do your taxes change,

00:00:52.886 --> 00:00:54.971
and what does it all mean for your income?

00:00:54.971 --> 00:00:57.140
- Jonathan Taylor Thomas.

00:00:57.140 --> 00:00:59.976
(uplifting music)

00:01:06.566 --> 00:01:09.069
- Divorce can be a wealth strangler.

00:01:09.069 --> 00:01:11.571
If you hire a divorce attorney
to handle all the nuts

00:01:11.571 --> 00:01:13.573
and bolts, you could easily drop tens

00:01:13.573 --> 00:01:16.326
of thousands of dollars on legal fees.

00:01:16.326 --> 00:01:17.410
Attorneys do a lot,

00:01:17.410 --> 00:01:20.955
like negotiating with your
spouse's lawyer on your behalf,

00:01:20.955 --> 00:01:22.916
drawing up paperwork and correctly filing

00:01:22.916 --> 00:01:26.711
so all of your divorce is
legal in the eyes of the law.

00:01:26.711 --> 00:01:28.004
- You'll probably keep more money

00:01:28.004 --> 00:01:29.964
in your pocket going through mediation.

00:01:29.964 --> 00:01:32.217
This involves hiring a neutral third party

00:01:32.217 --> 00:01:34.594
who is specially trained in divorce law

00:01:34.594 --> 00:01:37.347
and dispute resolution to
help you work out the details

00:01:37.347 --> 00:01:38.640
of the split.

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:40.683
Mediation is typically faster, cheaper,

00:01:40.683 --> 00:01:42.310
and less stressful,

00:01:42.310 --> 00:01:44.562
though it may entail
hiring some other experts

00:01:44.562 --> 00:01:46.815
like an appraiser to value your property,

00:01:46.815 --> 00:01:49.859
a social worker to weigh in
on child custody questions,

00:01:49.859 --> 00:01:51.194
and perhaps even a lawyer

00:01:51.194 --> 00:01:53.905
since mediators cannot give legal advice.

00:01:53.905 --> 00:01:55.865
Mediation can be a solid option

00:01:55.865 --> 00:01:58.284
if you have a straightforward
divorce where both parties

00:01:58.284 --> 00:02:00.286
are in agreement on most things.

00:02:00.286 --> 00:02:02.747
But if you're butting
heads and things drag on,

00:02:02.747 --> 00:02:04.874
the final bill can get hefty.

00:02:04.874 --> 00:02:07.210
- Divorce could throw
a lower earning spouse

00:02:07.210 --> 00:02:09.546
into financially choppy waters, especially

00:02:09.546 --> 00:02:11.881
if they've been a stay at home parent.

00:02:11.881 --> 00:02:14.384
All of a sudden they're on
their own to manage their money

00:02:14.384 --> 00:02:15.635
and keep up their household

00:02:15.635 --> 00:02:17.178
as a single person.

00:02:17.178 --> 00:02:20.056
Think of alimony, aka spousal support,

00:02:20.056 --> 00:02:22.016
as a temporary lifeline,

00:02:22.016 --> 00:02:24.185
one spouse provides financial support

00:02:24.185 --> 00:02:25.895
for a predetermined amount of time

00:02:25.895 --> 00:02:28.064
so that other can catch their breath.

00:02:28.064 --> 00:02:29.399
Every state has its own rules

00:02:29.399 --> 00:02:32.861
for determining alimony,
but both parties' income

00:02:32.861 --> 00:02:34.988
and expenses usually come into play.

00:02:34.988 --> 00:02:37.907
The idea is to enable
the lower earning spouse

00:02:37.907 --> 00:02:41.244
to maintain a similar standard
of living post divorce.

00:02:41.244 --> 00:02:44.164
- Raising kids comes with
a lot of expenses too.

00:02:44.164 --> 00:02:47.000
There's food, clothing,
housing, healthcare, activities,

00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:48.793
and so much more.

00:02:48.793 --> 00:02:50.420
Child support swoops in to make sure

00:02:50.420 --> 00:02:53.006
that each parent continues
paying their fair share

00:02:53.006 --> 00:02:54.674
after the divorce.

00:02:54.674 --> 00:02:56.009
Each state has its own guidelines

00:02:56.009 --> 00:02:58.219
for calculating it, but
certain factors tend

00:02:58.219 --> 00:03:00.305
to carry the most weight,

00:03:00.305 --> 00:03:02.891
namely your income, your ex's income,

00:03:02.891 --> 00:03:04.267
the number of children you have,

00:03:04.267 --> 00:03:06.811
their unique needs and
your custody arrangement.

00:03:06.811 --> 00:03:09.898
It's usually structured as a
monthly payment that fades out

00:03:09.898 --> 00:03:12.066
as each child comes of age.

00:03:12.066 --> 00:03:14.652
- Chances are going from
a two income household

00:03:14.652 --> 00:03:17.322
to the single life is going
to affect your lifestyle.

00:03:17.322 --> 00:03:19.657
If you own a home with your soon to be ex,

00:03:19.657 --> 00:03:22.452
you'll have to figure out
what to do with your digs.

00:03:22.452 --> 00:03:24.662
A property appraisal
can clarify the numbers.

00:03:24.662 --> 00:03:27.665
One of you could buy the
other out either with cash

00:03:27.665 --> 00:03:29.792
or by giving up other assets,

00:03:29.792 --> 00:03:32.295
like investments or retirement accounts.

00:03:32.295 --> 00:03:34.839
The one keeping the property
would then refinance

00:03:34.839 --> 00:03:36.883
the mortgage into their name,

00:03:36.883 --> 00:03:39.010
or you might agree to sell the property

00:03:39.010 --> 00:03:42.305
and split the profit 50/50
before going your separate ways.

00:03:42.305 --> 00:03:44.098
- How you split up your debt depends a lot

00:03:44.098 --> 00:03:45.808
on what state you live in.

00:03:45.808 --> 00:03:48.394
In some, a judge may decide
to use their own discretion

00:03:48.394 --> 00:03:51.522
to break up your debt based
on factors like income,

00:03:51.522 --> 00:03:53.274
employment and who accumulated it.

00:03:53.274 --> 00:03:54.442
In other states,

00:03:54.442 --> 00:03:57.278
marital debt is simply split
down the middle, and that's that.

00:03:57.278 --> 00:03:59.197
- Let's say you've each
got some student debt

00:03:59.197 --> 00:04:01.616
and credit cards, but one
spouse has way more debt

00:04:01.616 --> 00:04:03.117
than the other.

00:04:03.117 --> 00:04:05.370
Unless you can agree
on how to split it up,

00:04:05.370 --> 00:04:06.788
you'll have to go to the court

00:04:06.788 --> 00:04:09.457
and let the judge decide
based on state laws.

00:04:09.457 --> 00:04:12.919
- The IRS doesn't care if
you're married or divorced,

00:04:12.919 --> 00:04:14.754
they just wanna get paid.

00:04:14.754 --> 00:04:15.838
During tax time,

00:04:15.838 --> 00:04:18.424
you'll likely go from filing
jointly to doing it solo,

00:04:18.424 --> 00:04:21.135
sounds easy enough, but
things can get sticky

00:04:21.135 --> 00:04:22.720
the year you get divorced.

00:04:22.720 --> 00:04:23.596
Married couples benefit

00:04:23.596 --> 00:04:25.181
from certain tax breaks

00:04:25.181 --> 00:04:27.976
so your accountant might
actually suggest filing jointly

00:04:27.976 --> 00:04:30.436
if you're separated but not yet divorced.

00:04:30.436 --> 00:04:32.689
- When you do start filing
your taxes separately

00:04:32.689 --> 00:04:35.608
and you've got kiddos, you
guys will have to figure out

00:04:35.608 --> 00:04:37.443
who's claiming them as dependents.

00:04:37.443 --> 00:04:39.320
It's important because
it could open the door

00:04:39.320 --> 00:04:41.447
for some nice tax credits.

00:04:41.447 --> 00:04:44.617
- One other thing, alimony
and child support don't count

00:04:44.617 --> 00:04:46.494
as taxable income.

00:04:46.494 --> 00:04:48.538
Good news for the receiving spouse,

00:04:48.538 --> 00:04:50.164
but the spouse making these payments

00:04:50.164 --> 00:04:52.625
can't write it off as
a tax deduction either.

00:04:52.625 --> 00:04:55.044
Sorry, but that's the rule as of 2019.

00:04:55.044 --> 00:04:58.214
- Yikes, this stuff is
kind of a downer, huh?

00:04:58.214 --> 00:05:00.591
- Yeah, but it can also
be a teachable moment

00:05:00.591 --> 00:05:03.344
for couples who want to turn things around

00:05:03.344 --> 00:05:04.387
and grow together.

00:05:04.387 --> 00:05:06.347
- Couple's therapy can be a game changer.

00:05:06.347 --> 00:05:08.057
Think of it as routine maintenance

00:05:08.057 --> 00:05:10.476
to help keep your
relationship going strong.

00:05:10.476 --> 00:05:12.603
We've been going for 13 years,

00:05:12.603 --> 00:05:14.355
and it's made all the difference.

00:05:14.355 --> 00:05:16.190
- Or you might find that
you'd be truly happier

00:05:16.190 --> 00:05:18.776
starting a new chapter on your own.

00:05:18.776 --> 00:05:20.445
Maybe you'll meet somebody new

00:05:20.445 --> 00:05:23.156
and better like Jonathan Taylor Thomas

00:05:23.156 --> 00:05:25.158
- Save it for therapy.

